ic diseases that currently don't  have a treatment and in Steve's lab  we're trying to develop si RNA therapies  to treat these kinds of diseases so  those of you who aren't familiar with us  RNA I hope most of you are but just in  case you're not these are small  double-stranded RNAs that are able to  mediate silencing of virtually any gene  target in the human transcriptome there  are some great attributes that we think  make RNA an ideal drug people will say  drug the undruggable so you can actually  hit targets that aren't druggable by  conventional conventional small-molecule  means it's possible to target multiple  mRNAs and I think one of the most  interesting attributes of this kind of a  drug is that you can actually change the  sequence so there are some diseases that  will evade conventional therapies by by  a mutation right so cancer is a disease  that thrives on mutation influenza is a  disease that is known for mutation so si  RNA drugs in theory could evolve and  keep pace with those kinds of those  kinds of diseases there are some  drawbacks to this technology for one  thing for the last billion years our  cells have been evolving ways to keep  them out so SI rnas have 40 negatively  charged phosphate phosphate groups on  their backbones and that bounces them  right off the the cell membrane so  srna's are also rapidly degraded in the  serum and they are cleared by the  kidneys and they also trigger the innate  immune response in cells so there's like  all these negative drawbacks but one by  one in the dowdy lab we've developed  chemistry to address all of those things  and I can sum it up by saying we  developed by reversible neutral  phosphodiester backbone chemistry that  allows us to mask these molecules from  serum proteases allows them to bind to  serum albumin keep them from being  cleared and most importantly neutralize  some of those negative  charges so these molecules can be  delivered to to cells as long as they  have a targeting domain 